  "testbench": {
    "name": "comparator-preamp-noise",
    "description": "Input-referred noise of the comparator front end over PVT via ngspice .noise, reported as total integrated OUTPUT noise divided by the measured DC gain, split into a full band (1 Hz .. 1 GHz) and a >1 kHz band so the flicker content is visible rather than buried.",
    "claim": "sim/dut.json binds design/comparator.spice (id comparator-dr0001, provenance schematic), the ratified topology from spec/decision-records/0001-comparator-topology.md ('DR-0001'). This record substantiates that a .noise sweep over the full PVT grid genuinely runs and the divide-by-gain referral genuinely computes on that design -- but is STILL NOT evidence toward README.md#target-specification's Input-referred-noise row: that ratification is a separate, later act (spec/porting-plan.md's third step in this chain). It is ALSO a LOWER BOUND, not the design's real noise floor: per DR-0001 Decision Section 3, this topology has no DC-resolvable analog front end, so comparator_dut_analog (what this bench instantiates) is DR-0001's own named interim path, a diode-connected, loop-broken reduced sub-model that excludes the regenerative loop's own noise contribution entirely (design/README.md).",
    "experiment": "comparator-preamp-noise",
    "directory": "testbench",
    "netlist": "tb_preamp_noise.spice",
    "netlist_sha256": "16cb64f2711e37101a909c16fed05f5d72341b06c67cfeb8429a8773a5ce97ae",
    "manifest_sha256": "4310598c7c33ad6389bb02a63e9f452086e91766171abce041ea41478b857ac5",
    "nominal_supply_v": 1.2,
    "supply_tolerance": 0.1
  },
  "checks": {
    "av_dc": {
      "min": 1.0,
      "min_spread_pct_by_axis": {
        "process": 0.5,
        "temperature": 20.0
      },
      "description": "DC differential gain of the front end, and this bench's corner-sensitivity anchor. The floor of 1.0 asserts the stage is a gain stage at every corner -- a front end with gain below unity would make every input-referred number here larger than the output-referred one and the divide-by-gain step actively misleading. It is NOT a design bound on gain. The per-axis floors are what make --sabotage-corners fail: gain is (transconductance x load resistance) and both move with corner and temperature. The temperature floor (20.0) is still the value originally calibrated from this experiment's first committed 45-point record (named in this experiment's README.md, run --no-write during issue #8 development, against the then-bound placeholder DUT), ~48% margin below that record's observed weakest temperature slice (38.91%); it still holds against the real DR-0001 DUT (sim/dut.json now binds design/comparator.spice), whose own weakest observed temperature slice is 21.06% (record sim/comparator-preamp-noise/records/20260916-021939-36773c7.json). The process floor was RECALIBRATED (issue #16): the placeholder-derived value (3.0, ~48% margin below that placeholder record's observed weakest process slice of 5.89%) FAILED against the real DUT's own weakest observed process slice of 1.069% (same record) -- physically plausible, not a plumbing bug: comparator_dut_analog's DC gain is a gm-ratio between the input pair and its diode-connected loads (design/README.md), which partly cancels process skew to first order, unlike the placeholder's ideal-resistor-loaded front end. The new floor (0.5) is recalibrated from that same real-DUT record, ~53% margin below its observed weakest process slice (1.069%); see design/README.md's 'Open items' for the resolution note. The supply axis is unfloored: the front end is biased by a forced current, so its gain barely moves with the rail (observed weakest supply slice: 5.63%)."
    },
    "vn_in_uv": {
      "min": 0.1,
      "description": "THE HEADLINE NUMBER: total integrated output noise (1 Hz .. 1 GHz) DIVIDED BY THE MEASURED DC GAIN. The floor only catches a degenerate analysis (a `.noise` that returned nothing, or a gain so large the division collapses the result); the SPEC bound is deliberately not encoded while a placeholder is bound. Read the units caution in the testbench header: ngspice's own inoise_total is recorded separately as inoise_band_uv precisely so the two referrals can be compared rather than conflated."
    },
    "inoise_band_uv": {
      "min": 0.1,
      "description": "ngspice's OWN input referral over the same band, recorded alongside the divide-by-gain number rather than instead of it -- AND THE TWO ARE NOT EXPECTED TO AGREE. ngspice refers each frequency bin through the AC transfer function at THAT frequency, so every bin above the front end's bandwidth is divided by a rolled-off gain and inflates the total; the quoted number divides the whole integrated output noise by the DC gain instead."
    },
    "flicker_frac_pct": {
      "min": 0.0,
      "max": 100.0,
      "description": "Fraction of the total noise POWER contributed below 1 kHz, i.e. the flicker content. Bounded only to the range a fraction can legally take -- outside it means the two noise integrals came back inconsistent."
    },
    "enbw_mhz": {
      "min": 0.001,
      "description": "Equivalent noise bandwidth implied by (total integrated noise / white density). Lets a reader re-derive the headline number by hand as density x sqrt(ENBW) instead of taking the integral on trust. Floor only -- a zero or negative ENBW means the spectrum sample used as the white reference landed somewhere it should not have."
    },
    "vbias_anchor_mv": {
      "min": 50.0,
      "max": 1200.0,
      "description": "The same V_th-proxy anchor as sim/comparator-offset-mc/, measured on the DUT's ibias contract pin. Recorded here without per-axis floors because av_dc above already carries them for this bench; keeping the same anchor in both records makes the two directly comparable point by point."
    }
  },
